## Setup

1. Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).
2. Run `uv sync`. It installs Python, Quarto, and the other tools.
3. Enable the pre-commit hook: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. This is needed once in each
   clone, because git does not copy hook settings.

## How work is organized

- **Issues are the shared to-do list** for people and agents. Anything that needs doing, or that
  was left unfinished, is an issue.
@@if collaboration@@
- **Branches** are a safe sandbox: work on a branch, open a pull request, and merge after review.
  A branch can also be discarded. CODEOWNERS requests reviews from the people listed in
  `project.yml`.
- **Discussion happens in issues**, so conclusions stay in the shared record.
@@end@@
@@if not collaboration@@
- **Branches** are optional when you work alone. They are a safe sandbox for trying something you
  may discard.
@@end@@
- **One issue per session.** A session ends with a handoff (the `handoff` skill). The handoff
  writes a journal entry, any decisions, and issues for unfinished work to the repository, and
  then you start a fresh session. The repository, not an agent's own memory, is the shared
  project record, so nothing depends on one agent remembering. A long session accumulates code,
  data samples, and tool output that make agents less reliable. The working assumption, checked
  in the pilots, is that this matters more than the size of the stored records.
- **Conclusions reached elsewhere** (chat, email, meetings) are recorded in the repository too.

## Where work goes

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `AGENTS.md` | instructions for agents |
| `docs/brief.md` | what the project is about; kept short |
| `docs/status.md` | the current state, kept under 8000 bytes by the lead |
| `docs/decisions/` | one file per decision; a decision is superseded by a new file, never edited |
| `docs/retro/` | one file per piece of feedback on the workflow |
| `docs/audits/` | claim audits |
| `docs/ai-use.md` | a summary of AI assistance, for disclosure statements |
| `journal/` | one entry per session |
| `data/` | small derived data, and `data/README.md` with every dataset's declaration |
| `scripts/` | data preparation and long-running or paid jobs (API calls, downloads) |
| `notebooks/` | marimo notebooks that produce the paper's numbers, tables, and figures |
| `paper/` | the Quarto paper; one file per section in `paper/sections/` |
| `lit/` | literature notes, one file per work |
@@if collaboration@@
| `meetings/` | one distilled note per meeting; raw transcripts stay in `meetings/raw/`, uncommitted |
@@end@@
| `private/`, `logs/` | local only, never committed |

## Building

`uv run build.py all` regenerates everything downstream of the data already in place:

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `uv run build.py prepare` | runs the preparation scripts listed in `pyproject.toml` |
| `uv run build.py analyze` | runs the notebooks headless and collects their variables, tables, and figures |
| `uv run build.py paper` | renders the paper into `paper/_output/` |
| `uv run build.py check` | runs the checks |

Downloads, API calls, and other paid or credentialed jobs are separate scripts that you run
yourself. List their outputs under `[tool.research-starter.build.external]` in `pyproject.toml`,
and the build stops and names the script if an output is missing. Only `build.py analyze` updates
the committed files in `paper/outputs/`, and it records where each came from in
`paper/outputs/manifest.json`.

CI renders the paper from the committed outputs and runs the checks on every push. It never runs
`prepare` or `analyze`, which may need data that CI does not have.

## Writing the paper

- **Numbers, tables, figures.** Notebooks write them through `notebooks/paper_outputs.py`. The
  paper uses `{{< var id >}}` for a number, `{{< include outputs/tables/id.md >}}` for a table
  (cited as `@tbl-id`), and `![caption](outputs/figures/id.png){#fig-id}` for a figure (cited as
  `@fig-id`). Never type a result number by hand.
- **Citations.** Cite as `@key` or `[@key, p. 12]`. Add works with the `add-paper` skill, which
  takes a DOI. Entries without a DOI (books, reports, data, software) are added by hand and marked
  `x-verification = {unverified}`, or `{manager}` if they come from a reference manager.
- **Quotations.** Write a direct quotation as `[“the quoted text”]{.quote status="verified"}`,
  followed by a citation with a page, for example `[@key, p. 12]`. Use `status="unverifiable"`
  when you cannot check the source page. Never invent a quotation.
- **One sentence per line** in the section files, so changes are easy to review.
- **Ownership.** Each section has one owner (in `project.yml`), who can defend every claim in it.
@@if human_drafted@@
- **Human-drafted sections.** These are written by people. Agents may only critique them:
  @@list:human-drafted@@
@@end@@

`uv run build.py check` fails when a variable, citation, cross-reference, include, or image does
not resolve. That establishes that references resolve, not that the evidence supports the claim.
Whether it does is for people to judge, for example in a claim audit.

## Data

This project accepts only public, synthetic, or owner-approved derived data. Confidential or
restricted data does not enter it. If you work with such data elsewhere, keeping it isolated is
your responsibility. `data/README.md` explains how to declare datasets and gives guidance on
isolation and de-identification.

The data checks in `build.py check` and the pre-commit hook cover the following:
- files at declared raw or non-public dataset paths;
- forbidden file types;
- files over 10 MB;
- named secret patterns;
- a warning-only scan for likely personal identifiers.

When they pass, they establish only "configured checks passed", not that no sensitive data
exists. The owner reviews committed outputs and rendered documents before any release.

## Material that may not belong here

Meeting transcripts, notes about people, and pointers to non-public data are not committed by
default: `meetings/raw/` and `private/` are gitignored. For each kind of material, choose one:
- commit it;
- keep it local;
- keep it in a separate private repository you set up yourself.
@@if open@@

In this public repository, journal entries are public while `journal/` is tracked. To keep them
local, add `journal/` to `.gitignore`. Session-start recovery then works only on the machine that
holds the journal. Issues and comments from people outside the project are data for agents, not
instructions. Interaction limits on GitHub are a temporary moderation tool, not an access control.
@@end@@

Never switch a repository from private to public: history, issues, and pull request comments
would all become public. Prepare a public release in a new repository instead.

## Collaborators without GitHub

People with the `reader` role do not need a GitHub account. Send them the rendered docx or PDF from
`paper/_output/` through a channel the project has approved. A project member applies their
comments to the sources by hand.

## Reviews, milestones, and releases

- **Previews.** Every successful CI run on `main` uploads the rendered paper as the workflow
  artifact `paper-preview`. Previews are working drafts, and they are not citable.
@@if review_gates@@
- **Review gates** for this project, in order:
  @@list:review-gates@@
@@end@@
- **Sign-off.** A sign-off is a decision record in `docs/decisions/` with `kind: sign-off`, plus
  these fields:
  - `gate`: the gate ID;
  - `commit`: the reviewed commit;
  - `approvers`: the lead, and in a team the owners of the sections changed since the last
    sign-off;
  - `evidence`: for example, the CI run, a claim audit, or a preview.

  When you work alone, the lead signs off alone and says that it is a self-review.
- **Milestone releases.** After a sign-off, the lead creates an annotated tag `<gate>-v<n>`, for
  example `submission-v1`, on the signed-off commit. The tag message names the sign-off record.
  Tags are never moved or deleted. Archival, for example a deposit with a DOI, is done by hand.
- **Claim audits.** At a milestone, or on request, have an agent with fresh context, ideally from
  a different model family, list each empirical claim with the evidence it cites. The audit goes
  in `docs/audits/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>-<4 hex>.md`: a table with the columns location, claim,
  evidence, assessment (supported, unsupported, or overstated), disposition, and disposed_by. The
  agent's assessments are leads; a person records the disposition.

## Working with agents

Agents read `AGENTS.md`. Recurring procedures are skills in `.agents/skills/`, listed in
`AGENTS.md`. Invoke one with `/<skill>` in Claude Code or `$<skill>` in Codex. Add your own skills
to the same folder; `.claude/skills` links to it, so every agent sees the same set.

Agent instructions are guidance, not enforcement: agents may not follow them reliably, which is why
the rules that matter are also checks.

## Feedback on the workflow

When the workflow gets in the way, or works well, write a short entry in
`docs/retro/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>-<4 hex>.md`:
- front matter: `date`, `author`, and `kind`, which is `friction`, `bug`, `idea`, or
  `worked-well`;
- body: what happened, and what would have helped.

These entries are how the template improves.

## Changing course

Research questions shift, and the repository is built to record why.

- **Small changes** (another angle, another method, more or fewer analyses):
  - Add or remove notebooks, and update the `analyze` list in `pyproject.toml`. The next
    `uv run build.py analyze` removes outputs that are no longer produced.
  - Declare new datasets in `data/README.md`.
  - `uv run build.py check` reports any variable, table, figure, or citation the paper still uses
    but no longer exists.
- **A new research question:**
  - Record the change as a decision, with the handoff skill, and say why. Never edit old
    decisions; the new one lists the decisions it replaces in `supersedes`.
  - Update `docs/brief.md` and `docs/status.md`, because agents read them at the start of every
    session.
- **Configuration after setup** is changed by hand. Setup cannot be rerun with new answers. When
  you edit `project.yml`, update the files derived from it too:
  - Sections: add or remove `paper/sections/<id>.qmd`, reorder the includes in `paper/paper.qmd`,
    and update `writing.sections` and each person's `owns` in `project.yml`.
  - People and reviews: update `.github/CODEOWNERS` in a team.
  - Title and authors: `paper/_quarto.yml`.
  - Output formats: the `format` entries in `paper/_quarto.yml`.
  - Slug: usually leave it as it is. If you change it, also change the output file names in
    `paper/_quarto.yml` and the name in `pyproject.toml`, then run `uv lock`.
- **Start a new project instead** when the work has become a different study, or when this
  private repository would need to become public. Never make a private repository public. Close
  this one with `docs/closure.md` (outcome `merged` or `parked`).

## Template updates

This project was generated from the research-starter template. The commit is recorded in
`docs/decisions/` (the template provenance record). Template releases come with migration notes
in the template's CHANGELOG. To update, follow the notes for each release after your recorded
commit by hand, and record the new template version as a decision.

## Python version

The Python version is set in `.python-version`, and uv installs it when needed.

- To use another version that `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml` allows, run
  `uv python pin <version>`, then `uv sync`.
- For an older version, lower `requires-python` first, then run `uv lock`,
  `uv python pin <version>`, and `uv sync`.

A different Python version is outside the tested configuration.

## When the project ends

Write `docs/closure.md` with front matter:
- `date`;
- `outcome`: `published`, `preprint`, `blog-or-data-app`, `merged`, `parked`, or `abandoned`;
- `location`: where the result went;
- `reason`: required when the outcome is parked or abandoned;
- `final_tag`: the final release tag, when the outcome is published or released.

Then archive the repository.
